House Price Prediction Using Machine Learning
Project Summary

This project aimed to develop a predictive model for real-estate prices
utilizing machine-learning technology. The first stage consisted of
performing exploratory analysis as well as cleaning the input dataset.
Specifically, I searched for and removed extraneous, duplicate and/or
missing records from the data that would impact the validity of the
model; fortunately no issues were found and I did not have an
inordinate number of items to validate. Since the data encompasses
numerical and categorical variable types, I used One-Hot Encoding to
convert the categorical variables into numerical values prior to using
them in any machine learning model.

After the cleaning process was complete, I separated the data set into
80/20 splits (80% training and 20% test) for use in developing two
predictive models; Linear Regression and Random Forest Regressor. To
evaluate the accuracy of the predictions made by each of these models, I
measured absolute (mean absolute error - MAE), root mean square (root
mean square error - RMSE) and R² scores (a measure of how well the
regression line fits the actual data points). The linear regression model
yielded an R² score of approximately 0.65, while the random forest
model yielded an R² of 0.61. Therefore, the linear regression model
accounted for approximately 65% of the variation in price between
homes, with this model being the most accurate of the two.

To present the results, I created a number of graphs. The histogram
illustrates that there were more homes priced below and between 200k
than there were homes that were priced above the 200k price point. The
correlation matrix provided an indication of how the different variables
correlate to each other and which show a strong positive or negative
correlation. For instance, property tax has a strong negative correlation
to both housing type (single-family residence versus multi-family
residence), and housing price.
